
  First question, and it's the one the whole design hangs on. "Persist" and "track" can mean two quite different
  modules:

  - [1mA current-user store[0m — one live answer to "who is logged in right now," written at login, read by any other code,
    surviving page reloads. Other forms [3mread[0m from it. This is session/identity state; it's small, and it's what "other
    forms will need it later" most naturally describes.
  - [1mAn event trail[0m — an append-only record of things that happened ("user X logged in at T," later "user X submitted
    form Y"). Other forms [3mwrite[0m to it. This is audit/analytics; it grows without bound, needs a retention story, and
    usually needs somewhere off-device to go.

  They're not mutually exclusive — the trail usually sits on top of the store, since you need to know who the current
  user is to attribute an event to them. But building both when you need one is the expensive mistake here, and the
  store is the cheaper thing to change later; a trail with a storage location and a format is much harder to walk back
  once other code writes to it.

  My recommendation is the current-user store, because "other forms will need it later" reads like those forms want to
  [3mknow[0m the user, not report on them.
